1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,762
Here is one line of C.

2
00:00:07,762 --> 00:00:12,519
It adds the variables b and c and stores the sum in the variable a.

3
00:00:12,519 --> 00:00:19,333
But a CPU can't run C at all, because all it understands is long strings of zeros and ones.

4
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:22,365
Between the two there are two translation steps.

5
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:25,775
First the compiler turns the C into assembly language,

6
00:00:25,775 --> 00:00:32,028
and then the assembler turns each assembly instruction into one thirty-two bit machine instruction.

7
00:00:32,028 --> 00:00:40,000
Because the two match almost one to one, you can think of assembly as machine code written out as text.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,431
Every CPU comes with a rulebook that says which instructions it supports and which registers it has.

9
00:00:46,431 --> 00:00:51,061
That rulebook is called the instruction set architecture, ISA for short.

10
00:00:51,061 --> 00:00:56,400
It works like a contract, because software promises to use only those instructions,

11
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:01,233
and the hardware promises to carry them out correctly.

12
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:08,333
x86, ARM and RISC-V are all instruction set architectures, and this course uses RISC-V because it's open,

13
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:09,956
simple and very regular.

14
00:01:09,956 --> 00:01:15,568
The R in RISC stands for reduced, which means the instructions are few and uniform,

15
00:01:15,568 --> 00:01:18,205
so the hardware can be simple and fast.

16
00:01:18,205 --> 00:01:26,333
The base version, called RV32I, has only about forty instructions, and this series covers most of them.

17
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:30,298
In RISC-V, arithmetic instructions work only on registers,

18
00:01:30,298 --> 00:01:34,879
which are a small number of very fast storage cells inside the CPU.

19
00:01:34,879 --> 00:01:40,349
The base instruction set has thirty-two integer registers, named x0 through x31.

20
00:01:40,349 --> 00:01:49,066
Each one is thirty-two bits wide, and thirty-two bits, or four bytes, is what we call a word.

21
00:01:49,066 --> 00:01:50,502
So why only thirty-two?

22
00:01:50,502 --> 00:01:57,308
Because a smaller register file is faster, and a register can be read or written within a single clock cycle.

23
00:01:57,308 --> 00:02:04,966
Memory holds billions of bytes, but reaching into it can take a hundred times longer.

24
00:02:04,966 --> 00:02:08,691
Register x0 is special, because its value is always zero.

25
00:02:08,691 --> 00:02:13,266
If you try to write anything into it, the value is simply thrown away.

26
00:02:13,266 --> 00:02:20,900
That might sound wasteful, but you'll soon see how useful a register that is always zero can be.

27
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:24,980
In assembly we usually don't write x0 through x31.

28
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:29,387
Instead we use friendlier names, called the ABI names.

29
00:02:29,387 --> 00:02:35,916
The registers t0 through t6 are temporaries, s0 through s11 are saved registers,

30
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:40,433
and a0 through a7 hold arguments and return values.

31
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:43,822
A few others, like ra and sp, have special jobs.

32
00:02:43,822 --> 00:02:49,733
The rules for using them come with function calls in episode seven.

33
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:53,043
Arithmetic instructions all share one fixed format.

34
00:02:53,043 --> 00:02:59,533
First comes the name of the operation, then the destination register, and then two source registers.

35
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:06,600
So an add simply puts the sum of the two source registers into the destination register.

36
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:07,984
Here's an example.

37
00:03:07,984 --> 00:03:10,829
Say s1 holds seven and s2 holds five.

38
00:03:10,829 --> 00:03:19,900
When we run add s0, s1, s2, both values flow into the adder, and out comes twelve, which is written back into s0.

39
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:23,532
Subtraction works the same way, but now the order matters.

40
00:03:23,532 --> 00:03:30,045
The first source register is the number we subtract from, and the second is the number being subtracted,

41
00:03:30,045 --> 00:03:33,166
so seven minus five leaves two.

42
00:03:33,166 --> 00:03:39,908
Let's compile a slightly bigger expression, one that adds b and c and then subtracts d.

43
00:03:39,908 --> 00:03:48,266
We'll assume the compiler has already put the variables a, b, c and d into registers s0, s1, s2 and s3.

44
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:53,192
Each instruction can do only one operation, so we split this into two steps,

45
00:03:53,192 --> 00:03:57,200
and keep the middle result in the temporary register t0.

46
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,690
Let's say b is ten, c is twenty and d is five.

47
00:04:00,690 --> 00:04:05,015
The first instruction adds b and c, so t0 becomes thirty.

48
00:04:05,015 --> 00:04:12,466
The second one subtracts d from t0, which leaves twenty-five in s0, and we're done.

49
00:04:12,466 --> 00:04:15,963
What if the thing we want to add is a constant, like five?

50
00:04:15,963 --> 00:04:20,304
For that there's the instruction addi, where the i stands for immediate,

51
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:24,700
because the constant is stored right inside the instruction itself.

52
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:31,287
There's no subi in RISC-V, because subtracting a constant is the same as adding a negative one.

53
00:04:31,287 --> 00:04:34,477
The constant in addi is only twelve bits wide,

54
00:04:34,477 --> 00:04:40,301
so it can only hold values from about minus two thousand to about plus two thousand.

55
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:41,411
Why twelve bits?

56
00:04:41,411 --> 00:04:44,933
We'll find out in episode nine.

57
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:47,398
Now we can see what x0 is good for.

58
00:04:47,398 --> 00:04:54,229
The assembler offers a few pseudo-instructions, and it replaces each one with a real instruction.

59
00:04:54,229 --> 00:05:02,399
The mv instruction is just an add of zero, li starts from x0 and adds a constant, and nop writes its result into x0,

60
00:05:02,399 --> 00:05:04,600
so nothing changes at all.

61
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:10,726
With just one register that is always zero, we avoid needing several special-purpose instructions,

62
00:05:10,726 --> 00:05:14,400
and that is exactly the RISC way of thinking.

